# Implementation Plan: AI-Assisted Detection of Stealth Network Reconnaissance

This plan details the design and deployment steps to build a complete, highly professional, modular AI-powered Intrusion Detection System (IDS) focused on detecting stealth network reconnaissance (e.g., SYN scans, FIN scans, Xmas scans, slow scans).

The system features high-performance packet capture, feature extraction based on advanced network flow statistics, machine learning models (Supervised and Unsupervised), real-time detection pipelines, and a premium dark-themed Streamlit dashboard.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Privileges & Interface Configuration**
> To capture live network packets, Python scripts need raw socket capabilities. This can be run using `sudo` or by giving Python/Scapy specific capabilities (`cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin+eip`). The installation script will configure non-root tshark capture capabilities for ease of use.
> 
> Please ensure you have a network interface with active traffic (e.g., `eth0`, `wlan0`, or `lo` for local testing) to verify live captures. We will default to a auto-detection mechanism with a fallback to `lo`.

> [!NOTE]
> **Machine Learning Strategy**
> Since stealth reconnaissance often involves slow, low-profile probes, we will extract statistical flow features (e.g., SYN-to-ACK ratios, time variance, port randomness entropy) calculated over sliding time windows or session flow groupings. 
> We will implement both **Supervised models** (Random Forest, XGBoost) for classifying known scan patterns and **Unsupervised models** (Isolation Forest) to detect novel, zero-day stealth scanning techniques.

---

## Open Questions

None at this time. The project goals are clear and the environment is a Linux workspace which provides full access to powerful tools like `tshark`, `scapy`, and Python.

